data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- A Cas9 nuclease paired with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA) is delivered into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable \"molecular scissors\" that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In bacteria and archaea, CRISPR sequences derive from phage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phage DNA during later infections [2].\n- A linked 2021 headline describes a CRISPR breakthrough as opening the door to treating a broad array of diseases [4]."}

